cctext patch 0002: bounded dashing and flattening.

A dash pattern far finer than its path (stroke-dasharray="1e-38" on a
100 px line) made plutovg_path_traverse_dashed emit billions of segments
until the element array's int capacity overflowed (fuzz, UBSan). Past
1,000,000 segments (path length / pattern length x entries) the stroke is
drawn solid, as Skia does for over-dense dashes; a non-finite pattern,
length or offset is drawn solid too.

plutovg_path_traverse_flatten subdivided a cubic until flat or 31 levels
deep; with control points near FLT_MAX its flatness measures overflow to
inf / NaN, the test never passes, and one curve became 2^31 segments (a
16 s render, fuzz). A curve whose measures are not finite is emitted as
its chord. A huge finite curve (1e8 units) cannot get flat to 0.25 in
float precision either (2^27 segments): subdivision now stops at depth 16
(65536 segments a curve, enough for curves up to ~1e9 units), and one
flattening emits at most 1,048,576 segments; past that the remaining
curves are chords (a 3.3 s dashed arc now takes 60 ms). Flattening only
serves dashing and path extents: fills and undashed strokes hand curves
to the rasterizer and stroker as before.

Apply after 0001.

--- a/source/plutovg-path.c
+++ b/source/plutovg-path.c
@@ -2,6 +2,13 @@
